1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Every January, your Social Security check

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:03,780
gets a raise.

3
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:06,940
And every January, before that raise ever

4
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,100
reaches your bank, Medicare takes its cut.

5
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:13,700
This year, the cut is small.

6
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,460
Over 20 years, it isn't.

7
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:18,880
And whatever it takes, you'll have to find

8
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:19,980
somewhere else.

9
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:22,620
At an age when going back to work isn't an

10
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:26,220
option. The 2027 raise was announced on

11
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:27,820
October 14th.

12
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:30,260
So today, we run three numbers.

13
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:33,920
The raise, the cut, and the drift between

14
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:36,940
them. The slow one, the one nobody puts in

15
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,780
a headline. Then we put all three on top

16
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,180
of a real savings balance and see what

17
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,540
they do to the age the money runs out.

18
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,320
Every figure comes from Social Security,

19
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:50,200
Medicare, or their trustees with the date

20
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,340
set out loud.

21
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:53,760
It's education, not advice.

22
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:56,000
Your numbers will be your own.

23
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:59,280
Number one, the raise.

24
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:02,440
Social Security goes up each year with a

25
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:04,780
measure of inflation, the consumer price

26
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:06,340
index for wage earners.

27
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:08,940
The government compares prices from July

28
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,040
through September with the same three

29
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:12,380
months a year earlier.

30
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,440
And the difference becomes your cost of

31
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:15,440
living adjustment.

32
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:19,920
For 2027, that adjustment is 3.6%.

33
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:22,500
The average retired worker collects about

34
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,520
$2,071 a month this year.

35
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:29,940
A 3.6% raise adds about $75 a month.

36
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,400
That's real money.

37
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,440
Over a year, it's about $890.

38
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:37,380
It arrives automatically in the January

39
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:39,640
payment, and you don't have to apply for

40
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:43,140
it. For context, in their spring report,

41
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:45,440
Social Security's trustees projected a

42
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,280
2.7% raise for 2027.

43
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:50,840
And over the last 10 years, the raises

44
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:52,920
have ranged from a fraction of a percent

45
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:56,740
to 8.7%. A big raise usually means the

46
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:58,920
year before was hard on your budget.

47
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:01,500
It's catching up, not getting ahead.

48
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,300
Here's the part that matters for later.

49
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:07,220
The raise is a percentage of your check.

50
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,540
It isn't a percentage of your bills.

51
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,160
If the things you actually pay for rise

52
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,740
faster than prices in general, the raise

53
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:17,480
falls behind, even when the headline looks

54
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:20,680
generous. How to check yours?

55
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,760
In early December, Social Security posts

56
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:25,700
your new benefit amount in the message

57
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:28,080
center of your My Social Security account

58
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:30,280
at ssa .gov.

59
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,880
It shows the new gross amount before

60
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:35,660
anything comes out, which brings us to the

61
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:37,060
thing that comes out.

62
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:40,420
Number two, the cut.

63
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:42,660
If you're on Medicare and collecting

64
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:46,040
Social Security, your Part B premium, the

65
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:47,880
monthly charge for doctor visits and

66
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,140
outpatient care, comes straight out of

67
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,240
your check before it's deposited.

68
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:54,200
You never see that money.

69
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,780
For 2026, the standard Part B premium is

70
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:00,560
$202.90 a month.

71
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,740
The 2027 premium won't be official until

72
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:05,820
Medicare announces it, usually in

73
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,240
November. Medicare's own trustees

74
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:11,960
projected it at about $209.50.

75
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:15,620
That's about $6.60 more a month.

76
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:17,640
One more line on the cut.

77
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:20,420
Not everyone pays the standard premium.

78
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,040
If your income two years ago was above a

79
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:26,560
set level, about $109,000 for a single

80
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:30,340
filer in 2026, Medicare adds a surcharge

81
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:34,180
called IRMAA, the income -related

82
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,040
surcharge. It's based on that older tax

83
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:40,240
return. So, a one -time spike, a big

84
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:42,900
required withdrawal, a Roth conversion,

85
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,000
the sale of a house, can raise your

86
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:46,760
premium two years later.

87
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,020
For most viewers, it won't apply.

88
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:51,460
If it might, it's worth knowing before the

89
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:53,240
year you take a large withdrawal.

90
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:55,980
So this year, the math is gentle.

91
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:59,640
A raise of about $75, a cut of about

92
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:04,400
$6.60. You keep about $68 a month of the

93
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,000
increase. If that were the whole story,

94
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:08,680
this would be a short video.

95
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:10,380
How to check yours.

96
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:12,620
The notice from Social Security lists

97
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:13,860
three lines.

98
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:16,780
Your new benefit, the Medicare deduction,

99
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:19,400
and what actually lands in your account.

100
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,280
Write all three down.

101
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:22,440
Keep the paper.

102
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:24,480
You'll want last year's next to it.

103
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:28,020
Before we get to the slow part, one more

104
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:30,280
thing comes out of a raise, and it moves

105
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:32,520
even less than the premium does.

106
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:35,960
Part of your Social Security becomes

107
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:38,760
taxable once your income passes a line set

108
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:41,640
by law. For a single filer, that line

109
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:44,980
starts at $25,000, counting half your

110
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,020
benefit plus your other income.

111
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:50,960
Above $34,000, up to 85% of your benefit

112
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:52,020
can be taxed.

113
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:56,040
Those lines were written in 1983 and 1993.

114
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:58,120
They have never been adjusted for

115
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:00,040
inflation, not once.

116
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:02,580
So every raise pushes a little more of

117
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:05,380
your benefit over a line that never moves.

118
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:07,740
A raise that keeps pace with prices can

119
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:10,180
still cost you more in tax than it did the

120
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:11,140
year before.

121
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:13,460
There is a temporary extra deduction right

122
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:17,420
now, up to $6,000 for people 65 and over,

123
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:20,280
through the 2028 tax year, and it phases

124
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:21,800
out at higher incomes.

125
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:24,860
It helps. It doesn't move the lines, and

126
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:26,340
it's scheduled to end.

127
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,240
How to check yours.

128
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:31,840
On your federal return, Line 6A is your

129
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:34,700
total benefit, and Line 6B is the part

130
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:35,800
that was taxed.

131
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:39,060
Compare 6B across the last three years.

132
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:41,420
If it's climbing faster than your benefit,

133
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:43,380
the lines are catching you.

134
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:47,060
Number 3. The Drift.

135
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,680
Here's what 10 years of those notices

136
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:50,960
would show you.

137
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:53,380
Over the last decade, the cost of living

138
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:56,220
raises added up to about 35%.

139
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:00,340
A check of about $1,500 in 2016 grew to

140
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:02,900
about $2,071 today.

141
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:05,620
Over the same 10 years, the standard Part

142
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:12,300
B premium went from $121.80 to $202.90,

143
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:17,020
up 67%, nearly twice as fast as the raise.

144
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,700
That's the drift.

145
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:20,660
Not a scandal, not a trick.

146
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,080
Healthcare costs have simply been rising

147
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:25,420
faster than prices in general for a long

148
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:28,020
time, and the premium follows the cost of

149
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:30,660
the care. The trustees expect Part B

150
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:32,740
spending to keep climbing quickly over the

151
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:33,980
next five years.

152
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:36,660
Now carry that forward.

153
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:40,480
Today, Part B takes about $1 in every 10

154
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:41,940
from the average check.

155
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:45,160
Let's assume the raise averages 2.4% a

156
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:47,260
year, which is what Social Security's

157
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:48,400
trustees project.

158
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,700
And let's assume the premium keeps rising

159
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:52,960
the way it did over the last 10 years,

160
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:54,660
about 5% a year.

161
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:59,500
By 2046, Part B takes about $1 in every 6.

162
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:03,400
At a faster 6.5%, the cautious case, it's

163
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:04,760
about 1 in 5.

164
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:07,220
Here's what that looks like in dollars, at

165
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:08,600
the 5% pace.

166
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:11,580
At 70, the part of the premium that outran

167
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:14,680
the raise costs about $300 that year.

168
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,220
At 75, about $900.

169
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:19,760
At 80, about $1600.

170
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:23,980
At 85, nearly $2700 a year.

171
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:26,760
At the faster pace, the 85 -year -old's

172
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:29,180
number is closer to $4400.

173
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:31,500
For a married couple both on Medicare,

174
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:34,200
that's two premiums drifting at once,

175
00:07:34,260 --> 00:07:36,360
usually paid out of one shared pool of

176
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:39,200
savings. Small, every year.

177
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:41,060
Then, not small.

178
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,300
And it arrives exactly when most people

179
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:45,260
are least able to earn their way around

180
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:48,780
it. Same check, same raises, a bigger

181
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:50,920
slice gone before the deposit.

182
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:53,100
And that's only the standard premium.

183
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:55,860
It's not the copays, not the drug plan,

184
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:56,880
not the dentist.

185
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:01,580
So where does the difference come from?

186
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:03,900
For most people living on Social Security

187
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:06,720
plus savings, the check covers the basics,

188
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:08,500
and savings cover the rest.

189
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:10,520
When a bigger share of the check goes to

190
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:12,920
Medicare, the basics don't shrink.

191
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:14,980
The gap moves onto the savings.

192
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:16,460
Let's run it.

193
00:08:16,580 --> 00:08:18,600
Same example as our Medicare episode.

194
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:23,480
Single, 66, $350,000 saved.

195
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:27,360
Pulling $14,000 a year to start, rising 3%

196
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:28,660
a year with inflation.

197
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:30,800
Savings earning 5%.

198
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:33,260
With no health costs at all, that money

199
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:35,100
would last until about $103.

200
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:38,800
Now add only the drift, just the part of

201
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:41,020
the Part B premium that grows faster than

202
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:43,320
the raise, paid out of savings each year.

203
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:47,180
At 5% premium growth, the money runs dry

204
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:51,440
at $99. At 6.5%, $97.

205
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:56,980
Run it again at a more cautious 4% return.

206
00:08:57,220 --> 00:09:00,120
With no drift, the money lasts to $96.

207
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:05,520
With the drift, $94, or $93, 2 to 6 years

208
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:08,360
from one line on one notice.

209
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:10,940
Before a single copay, before a single

210
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:11,700
prescription.

211
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:13,880
That's why the raise in the headline is

212
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:15,520
the wrong number to watch.

213
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:17,800
The number to watch is what's left after

214
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:20,340
the cut and how fast that's changing.

215
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:21,700
How to check yours.

216
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:24,360
Take your last few notices or your bank

217
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:26,200
deposits from each January.

218
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:28,160
Subtract the deposit from the gross

219
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:30,820
benefit. That's your Medicare deduction.

220
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:33,160
If it's growing faster than your raise,

221
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:35,380
the gap is coming out of your savings,

222
00:09:35,480 --> 00:09:37,360
whether you plan for it or not.

223
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:40,560
So, three numbers.

224
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:45,760
The raise. For 2027, 3.6%.

225
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:49,260
About $75 a month on the average check.

226
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:52,980
The cut. Medicare's Part B premium.

227
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:56,440
Projected at about $6.60 more.

228
00:09:56,640 --> 00:09:58,180
Taken before the deposit.

229
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,000
And the drift.

230
00:10:00,380 --> 00:10:02,820
For the last 10 years, the cut has grown

231
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:04,980
nearly twice as fast as the raise.

232
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,940
Carried forward, it pulls the age the

233
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:10,580
money runs out in by two to six years in

234
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:12,800
our example, from the standard premium

235
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:15,560
alone. Who does this hit hardest?

236
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:18,260
The people who rely on Social Security for

237
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:20,640
most of their income, and the people with

238
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:23,060
smaller savings to absorb the gap.

239
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:25,640
A larger balance hides the drift for

240
00:10:25,660 --> 00:10:28,080
longer. It doesn't stop it.

241
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:30,680
None of this means Medicare is a bad deal

242
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:32,280
or the raise is a sham.

243
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:35,200
Both are doing what they were built to do.

244
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:37,520
It means the raise was never designed to

245
00:10:37,540 --> 00:10:38,880
keep up with healthcare.

246
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:41,680
That part is on you to plan for.

247
00:10:41,940 --> 00:10:43,660
And remember where this started.

248
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:46,220
The raise is real, and you should be glad

249
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:48,060
of it. It's the headline.

250
00:10:48,360 --> 00:10:50,380
The deposit is the story.

251
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:52,700
One thing to do this year.

252
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:54,920
When your notice arrives in December,

253
00:10:55,020 --> 00:10:56,660
write down three numbers.

254
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:59,280
The new benefit, the Medicare deduction,

255
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:00,320
and the deposit.

256
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:02,740
Put them next to last year's.

257
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:04,120
Do it every year.

258
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:06,600
In five years, you'll see your own drift

259
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:08,320
in your own handwriting.

260
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,960
A fee only fiduciary or a CPA can put that

261
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:14,240
drift into your own plan with you.

262
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:15,600
That's what they're for.

263
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:17,840
Because the question was never whether you

264
00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:18,860
got a raise.

265
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,140
It's whether the money you saved is still

266
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:23,500
there at 90, when the check is smaller

267
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:25,760
than the bills and you can't go back to

268
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:28,860
work. If this was useful, subscribe.

269
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:31,540
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

270
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:35,160
Will your money last as long as you do?
